Z||000005||Formula88||09-21-2001||08:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>Did that really happen?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>While I can't personally verifiy that this is true, the friend of mine in Portland who forwarded it said she's known the fellow who wrote this for the last 10 years, and that she'd call  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> if she'd heard it from anyone but him.  <P>I'd like to think it's true.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||09-21-2001||08:31 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>I think I'll enjoy flying in the future, it's comforting knowing that airline security is being improved so much..<P>now all they need are massive parachutes to depoy if a plane loses power and starts to fall.. seriously, that should be feasible..<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Saw a show (discovery, I think) about this<BR>a couple of yrs ago. It's been perfected for <BR>ultra lights and tested on small civil aviation types. One small a/c company was going to offer it as an option, but I haven't heard anymore.Computer tested on 747's. It would work, but cost is prohibitive, especially with the airlines in such disarray now. I think airspeed would have to be pretty slow to keep from ripping the 'chutes apart.<BR>Note: This same email message is circulating around, supposedly from the captains of different air carriers. May be a made up thing or something the pilot's union has decided would be a good idea for all pilots to announce before takeoff.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||09-21-2001||08:48 PM||||As long as the pilot has control of the cockpit they can reduce cabin pressure and climb and everybody goes to sleep. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||09-22-2001||12:07 AM||||regarding the idea of reducing the oxygen to the cabin.. there'd be problems if that happened and an old person or baby died as a result..<P>they'd need some sort of release form that passengers would sign before the flight maybe<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||09-22-2001||03:56 AM||||Phil, the ruptured micro-blood vessels and possible circulatory problems (aneurisms, emoblisms etc) as well as the "bends" would make that a fairly unsafe idea. A better one would be to have a couple of tanks of Ether on the plane. Hit the big BLUE button, and everyone instantly starts getting insanely happy, before falling into a nice, soft, warm..., blue? ... sleep *yawn*<P>Just make everyone sign a waiver, and make sure that there aren't any built-in allergies (required med docs to fly etc) and no-one can sue, and no-one would get hurt.<P>Honestly, if that sort of thing happened, I would have sat in my seat right until the end. Before the 11th, the SOP was to go where they wanted, and discuss terms there. My own life as a hostage would have an inherent monetary value. <BR>Now? I'm trained in several techniques involving assailants with knives, some cases multiple assailants. I'd like to think that I'd be willing to risk all to take back the plane. The first words out of my mouth are "Hey look, 4 of them, 300 of us. Let's show them what colors their respective kidneys are!"<P>Oh, and that sounds pretty contrived, too, but the message is clear. SOP for hijackings has changed. I'd hope that anyone that knows how to fight would rise up and give them an airplane toilet swirly.||||reg||14
Z||000010||dennis_6||09-22-2001||04:23 AM||||I think instead of hiring air marshalls the airlines should give police or military personel free tickets and a weapon during the flight.  That way you would never know if an enforcement person was onboard.<P>                     ||||reg||3
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||09-22-2001||12:19 PM||||as a deterrant, that would be a good idea.. but regular military and police aren't trained for airplane combat situations.. at best it'd be a short term solution until people could be trained.. combat in an aircraft is a lot different than anywhere else (except maybe a spaceship or submarine, but that's another story..) so traditional techniques won't work. Guns aren't safe, they could depressurize the cabin and bring the plane down.. only thing left really is hand to hand combat, and simpler weapons like knives, or perhaps tasers..<P>I'd be cool with an ether release system, as long as the terrorists didn't have gas masks..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000012||theogre||09-22-2001||08:08 PM||||I strongly recomend anyone flying keep the seat belt on at all times. There are a number of things the crew can do if there's trouble and you won't stay in your seat without the belt if they happen.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||10:39 PM||||Steve--They have airplane safe bullets.  I just read about this the other day.  They will break skin, but not penetrate the shell of the airplane.  Seems like I read that they were plastic but that could be my imagination.  These are not the rubber riot bullets.  Apparently this is what is being carried by the undercover airline security (on board).  <P>Another airline safety note.  A company has developed a bullet/axe/grenade resistant cockpit door.  (The current doors are decorative only according to the FAA.  A lot of ****ing good that does anyone!)  These new doors are supposed to be around $600/each and some of the airlines don't want to spend that kind of money.  I guess they'd rather lose a multi million dollar airplane and countless innocent lives.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Formula88||09-23-2001||01:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Phil, the ruptured micro-blood vessels and possible circulatory problems (aneurisms, emoblisms etc) as well as the "bends" would make that a fairly unsafe idea. A better one would be to have a couple of tanks of Ether on the plane. Hit the big BLUE button, and everyone instantly starts getting insanely happy, before falling into a nice, soft, warm..., blue? ... sleep *yawn*<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's safer than a hijacking.  And do you really want Ether on a plane?  Just another highly combustible substance to worry about.  Fill the cabin with Ether - then all the terrorist does is flick his bic before passing out and BOOM - no more plane.  I'll risk the ruptured blood vessles, thank you.||||reg||6
Z||000015||Oreif||09-23-2001||01:24 AM||||Depressureizing the cabin also has another advantage. As soon as the cabin pressure is changed everyone becomes disorientated. Those sitting down would just get slightly dizzy and pass out. The terrorists would be unable to see anything let alone try and injure a hostage then pass out. Yes it would be a risk to some people who might be on the plane, But there would be less hijackings. The only way to get around it would be to have your own oxygen mask. I think the recent airport security people would be very curious if someone had an oxygen mask in their luggage. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||09-23-2001||05:04 PM||||Ether was the only anasthetic I could think about on hand, and know how to spell  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ... <BR>I'm not sure that the ppm of Ether needed to cause the desired effects would be enough to ignite an explosion. but I'm not sure.<BR>Any number of substitutes could work. Give the pilot total control of the passengers, and there wouldn't be any problems.||||reg||12
Z||000017||JSocha||09-24-2001||01:18 AM||||I'm really, really, really impressed with airport security now as a recent person who flew from Orlando to Memphis to Dallas after this incident.  I was anticipating the possibility of a "proctology" exam before I boarded any flights with the so called security measures that were in place.<P>Saturday, Sept. 15th:  10:35AM departure from Orlando.<P>Arrived at 6:30/7:00 AM stood in line for about an hour, checked my two suit cases in at the counter to go into the luggage compartment of the jet with a carry on camera bag that I could not lock.  At the counter, I was asked "Do you have anything to declare? Any weapons?".<P>Answer was "NO".<BR>Response, "Okay! Thank you! Your gate is that way".<P>Camera bag and myself went through the scanners and the change in my pocket and my cell phone battery did not even set off the alarm like it typically does, and yet, they are apparently set now super sensitive and can apparently pick up the metal buttons on your clothing.  I was quite impressed.  Not even the hand held wands went off.<P>Didn't even empty my pockets!  I was cleared!<P>I can not remember if it was in Orlando or Texas, but a news reporter put a "lipstick" camera in his carry on luggage and showed that security hadn't really changed.<P>His bag made it through the scanner and on the other side, you seen security going through somebody elses carry on bag, a security guard looking all over the place and the reporter made it on board with a finger nail clipper and pocket knife.<P>He said the only drastic change was the silverware.  Spoon, forks and a "plastic" knife were presented with the onflight meal.  <P>Anyone trained in military style tactics can tell you that even the spoon, fork and plastic knife are all "potential and deadly weapons" to those that know how to use them.  Even I was trained in my martial arts classes how to use the simplest of items within reach as a weapon right down to a twig and/or a stone in an effective manner.<P>Tuesday, Sept. 18th:  Getting ready to leave Dallas.  Got up at 6:00 AM and was watching the local Dallas news.<P>I only caught the tail end of the story as I was getting ready to leave back to ND, but they were considering on shutting down Dallas/Fort Worth (DFW) because they had a guy in custody/detained who had gotten a .38 revolver aboard via his carry on luggage.  He made it through security and entirely to his destination where a passenger that he had been bragging this to reported him to security once they landed.  A little late in my opinion if you ask me.<P>I just don't know if he had gotten on at DFW and made it to his final destination or if DFW was his final stop from someplace else.  Just made me glad to know that I was driving home hearing how lax airline security still was.<P>JohnF, IwannaIRM, Loki...what did happen?  Been curious since I left.  Can you fill me in on the details or the others here?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||9
